```

The concrete statements are `DELETE FROM memo` (`store/db/sqlite/memo.go:237`, `store/db/postgres/memo.go:244`, `store/db/mysql/memo.go:259`), `DELETE FROM memo_relation` (`store/db/sqlite/memo_relation.go:137` and peers), and `DELETE FROM attachment` (`store/db/sqlite/attachment.go:211` and peers). `store.DeleteAttachment` also removes the underlying local/S3 file (`store/attachment.go:132`), which is storage, not a table.

Two caveats worth knowing:

`memo_share` declares `FOREIGN KEY (memo_id) REFERENCES memo(id) ON DELETE CASCADE` in all three schemas (`store/migration/sqlite/LATEST.sql:109`, `store/migration/postgres/LATEST.sql:108`, `store/migration/mysql/LATEST.sql:108`), and no Go code deletes shares when a memo is deleted. So shares are cleaned up by the engine on Postgres and MySQL, but the SQLite driver opens the connection with foreign keys turned off, so share rows are orphaned there:

```53:53:store/db/sqlite/sqlite.go
	sqliteDB, err := sql.Open("sqlite", profile.DSN+"?_pragma=foreign_keys(0)&_pragma=busy_timeout(10000)&_pragma=journal_mode(WAL)&_pragma=mmap_size(0)")
```

`reaction` rows are listed at `server/router/api/v1/memo_service.go:545` purely to build the webhook payload, and `Store.DeleteReaction` (`store/reaction.go:39`) is called only from the `DeleteMemoReaction` endpoint (`server/router/api/v1/reaction_service.go:141`). Since reactions are keyed by the string `content_id` rather than a `memo_id` foreign key, they are left behind after a memo is deleted.

Also note the comment cleanup is one level deep only: comments-of-comments are not visited, so nested comment memos keep their rows.
